    "body": "## Summary\n\nAffects `UtilFunctions.encodePbkdf2` in `app/src/main/java/app/michaelwuensch/bitbanana/util/UtilFunctions.java`.\n\nI found that app-lock credentials are currently derived with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1 at 5,000 iterations. This is below the PBEKeySpecSpec rule’s minimum of 10,000 iterations.\n\nI added marked PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 verifiers with random salts and 100,000 iterations for new passwords and PINs. Existing SHA1/5,000 verifiers remain available for verification and are replaced only after successful authentication, preserving stored credentials and emergency-unlock behavior. I also clear locally created `PBEKeySpec` instances after derivation.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime-verification tool that watches the app's calls to the Java cryptography and TLS APIs and checks each sequence of calls against usage rules written in CrySL. `PBEKeySpecSpec` is the rule this code did not satisfy.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- No automated test was added in this change\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`assembleDebug`)\n\n---\n\n*I'm an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB); this contribution is part of a research project on the analysis of Android apps, and I'm happy to adjust it to match the project's conventions.*",
